cartridge "DRAGON'S DEN/COMPUTE!'S GAZETTE/1985"

' DRAGON'S DEN
' A ColecoVision game based on "Dragon's Den", COMPUTE!'s Gazette disk,
' December 1985 (Commodore 64, BASIC; no author is named on the listing).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you are lost in a dragon's den, a grid of 9 x 10 chambers,
' seen in first person. Each chamber shows its openings to the left,
' ahead and to the right; you can always go back the way you came. Some
' chambers have a trap door in the floor: search it, and you may find
' nothing, water, a one-shot magic sword, gold (once you hold the sword),
' or the rattle of scales: a dragon is under you, RUN through any opening
' at once or meet it. With the sword you slay it (the sword is spent);
' without, you are eaten. Searched deep enough (20 trap doors), a trap
' door can be an exit: escape with your gold, or hunt on. Every step and
' every search makes you thirstier (100 at most, water resets it); bread
' marks a chamber (20 slices; none left and you starve). The chart shows
' the chambers you have left behind, your marks and the trap doors opened.
'
' From the original (orig/dragonsden.bas): the 9 x 10 grid, chamber kinds
' 1-7 (L A R, A R, R, L A, A + trap, L R + trap, L + trap) seen relative
' to the way you face, moves LEFT, AHEAD, RIGHT (turn and step) and BACK
' (always allowed), the edge of the grid a dead end where only BACK works,
' the five clue messages (smoke, soot, dripping water, blood, bats) for
' clue digits 1-5 of 8, the search outcomes 1-9 (nothing 1 and 4, scales
' 2 3 6 8, exit check 5, water 7, sword-or-gold 9), the exit after 20
' searches (else scales), ESCAPE or HUNT (hunting sets the count to 5),
' the sword killing one dragon, thirst over 100 moves, 20 bread slices
' used by MARK, the chart (L visited, M marked, you), the start at a
' random chamber facing "up", the gold count in the escape message.
'
' Liberties: the joystick picks the move (left, up = ahead, right, down =
' back), the left button searches, the right button opens a small menu
' (CHART, MARK, CANCEL). The run from a dragon lasts 1.5 seconds (the C64
' gave about ten keyboard polls, a fraction of a second). A trap door can
' be searched once; it stays open for good (the original let you search
' the same trap door again and again without moving, which made standing
' still the best plan); so that enough trap doors remain, the den always
' has at least 34 of them, and once the exit is due outcomes 1 and 4
' (nothing) also find it. The clues mean something: dripping water over
' a trap door means water below, blood means a dragon (in the original
' the clue digit was unrelated to the search). A chamber is marked visited
' when you leave it (as in the original) and the chart also shows the
' trap doors you opened. Marking a chamber already marked costs nothing.
' The escape and death screens keep the den in view; no TRY AGAIN.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.
' Own xorshift random numbers. Nothing flashes.

const TBlank = 128
const TV0 = 129
const TV1 = 130
const TV2 = 131
